  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Image**\n\nI\'m focused on \"media_1792862435168_88c35dd7.png\" right now. It\'s clear that the \"Opening Hero\" contains two full-screen stages. I\'ve begun to break down \"Stage A\" within this structure. There might be some important implications.\n\n\n**Exploring Visual Hierarchy**\n\nI\'ve moved on to Stage B, the macro image of skin/lips. This full-screen visual is immediately below Stage A. There\'s no background text, but the horizon bar remains. I\'m noting the absence of white background here, as it\'s a key element. It will also be important to understand the design decisions about the images.\n\n\n**Clarifying Intentions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the client\'s direct request. It seems they want the new macro skin image to be full-screen in Stage B, but without the white background or the text overlay that was present on the previous image. The white background should be deferred. This clarification is helping me refine my understanding of the desired visual flow.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Structure**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the nuances of Stage B. The client\'s desire for a full-screen, edge-to-edge macro image without the white background is clear. My focus is on ensuring this image is seamless, with only the NOVA horizon line. The key is to start the white background later, not in this stage. I\'m focusing on preserving the macro detail without any distractions.\n\n\n**Implementing Scroll Logic**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the scroll behavior for the white header and background. The white navbar should be hidden during Stages A and B. It must only appear after scrolling past both. The warm white background debuts only after the Stage B image. I\'ll update the component logic to reflect this. I\'ve been checking media_1792862435168_88c35dd7.png, and have confirmed that the warm white background starts after stage B. I\'ll focus now on adjusting App.tsx and Header.tsx to achieve this visual flow.\n\n\n"
