  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering CSS Impact**\n\nI\'ve just pinpointed something in `/src/index.css`. Line 20 reveals the foundational font family and background color. This directly impacts the initial user experience; \'Basel Grotesk\' is the primary font, and a variable dictates the background. This immediately frames further inquiry.\n\n\n**Analyzing Overflow Solutions**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the implications of `overflow-x: hidden` in `/src/index.css`, and contrasting it with `overflow-x: clip`. It seems setting `overflow-x: hidden` on the `body` is problematic, potentially breaking `position: sticky`. It appears `overflow-x: clip` might be a better solution. Further, I am considering integrating GSAP ScrollTrigger for a scrub timeline on the `philosophy-dual-video-grid`, providing controlled animations during scroll.\n\n\n**Refining ScrollTrigger Logic**\n\nI\'m currently perfecting the GSAP ScrollTrigger implementation. The goal is precise control over the `philosophy-small-sticky-card`\'s vertical translation during scroll. I am calculating `start` and `end` points to perfectly scrub `y` from `0` to `largeVideoHeight - smallCardHeight`. This avoids the limitations and inconsistencies of CSS `sticky` and `overflow-x: hidden`. The aim is smoother, more predictable animation tied to the viewport.\n\n\n**Optimizing Visual Proportions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual proportions to enhance verticality. Currently, the larger video\'s aspect ratio, defined in `/src/App.tsx`, feels off. I\'m exploring an adjustment from `lg:h-[132vh]` and `lg:col-span-5` to achieve a truly vertical feel. I\'m targeting a 3:4.6 aspect ratio to improve the tall vertical column. Also, I am ensuring the small card scrolls to the bottom edge.\n\n\n**Calculating Vertical Alignment**\n\nI\'ve been working on getting the aspect ratios just right. I am now trying `aspect-[3/4.5]` along with `lg:aspect-auto` and `lg:min-h-[1120px]` for a more vertical look and a smoother scroll animation, especially to keep the small card and caption within the viewport. I\'m focusing on making the larger video vertically dominant.\n\n\n**Refining Scroll Behavior**\n\nI\'m now integrating GSAP and considering dynamic start and end points for the ScrollTrigger animation. The goal is precise control as the small video scrolls with the big video. I am analyzing viewport percentages, especially top and bottom, relative to the grid\'s positions on scroll. My focus is on ensuring a smooth user experience.\n\n\n**Calculating Scroll Boundaries**\n\nI am working through the scroll boundaries. I\'ve been calculating viewport percentages to ensure smooth transitions. Specifically, I am analyzing what happens as the user scrolls, given the `largeEl` and `smallEl` dimensions. Currently I am tracing the start and end points of the small element\'s scroll to ensure it stays in view. I have the start and end points: start at `top 112px`, and end at `bottom bottom-=80`.\n\n\n**Verifying Scroll Calculations**\n\nI\'m now running calculations on the scroll behavior. I\'ve confirmed that at the animation\'s start, `smallEl`\'s top is at 120px in the viewport, and at the end, it\'s at 425px. The large element scrolls 630px, while the small element moves 935px relative to the large one. This ensures precise alignment as the small video moves from the top to the bottom relative to the large video.\n\n\n**Adjusting Video Visibility**\n\nI\'m now refining the start and end trigger points. I\'m exploring an earlier start (`top 55%`) and a later end (`bottom 75%`) for the big video\'s animation. I\'m also checking how the start at `top 45%` and end at `bottom 82%` affects scroll behavior. This focuses on smoother movement within the viewport.\n\n\n\n**Confirming Visual Alignment**\n\nI\'ve just validated calculations. At start (`top 45%`): the small element\'s viewport top is 405px. Midway, it\'s 387px. At the animation\'s end (`bottom 82%`), it\'s 323px. The small video and caption will stay centered, while traveling the big video\'s full length, ensuring alignment throughout the scroll. I feel that these numbers are great!\n\n\n**Evaluating Sticky Performance**\n\nI am now comparing the performance of GSAP ScrollTrigger to CSS sticky, specifically the implementation of `top-24`. CSS `sticky` was problematic: it started too late and ended too early, not aligning correctly. GSAP with `ScrollTrigger` is superior because it starts when the dual-video block is mid-screen, and travels smoothly to the bottom. In addition, I will be updating `/src/index.css` to use `overflow-x: clip` instead of `overflow-x: hidden` for a cleaner layout.\n\n\n"
